# Inputs still needed for the full research plan

This package runs the existing-data analyses now and inventories likely local
sources. It does not silently replace ordinary-game denominators with timeout
choices, extrapolate game accuracy from a terminal score, or launch millions
of engine evaluations. Read `00_inventory/optional_source_inventory.json` in
the return ZIP to bind a subsequent builder to the actual schemas and archives.

## 0.1 All-game account markers

A separate account-game scan must retain both players' usernames/IDs, start
and end times, rated flag, speed/rating pool, pregame rating, provisional flag,
termination status, winner, disconnected role where identifiable, and engine
evaluation at the final recorded position with orientation/mate metadata.

For each account-year, excluding both author accounts in both roles:

- Count all eligible games, rated and casual counts, and all timeout games in
  which the account was the disconnected party. Own-disconnect rate has an
  all-game denominator; the chosen scope and coverage must be explicit.
- Among own rated losses whose own final evaluation <= -500 cp, report
  resignation, outoftime, mate and timeout/abandonment counts and shares.
  Missing evaluations are unknown losses, not non-resignations. Report engine
  coverage by status, speed and year before interpreting the share.
- Compute rating level and within-year SD within a rating pool. Never mix
  bullet and classical ratings into the requested all-game volatility measure.
  Record provisional status at a defined time and account age from creation.
- Use no granting/receiving behavior in construction of these external markers.
  The later selected Patron snapshot is its own measurement/sampling frame.

The current runner supplies explicitly named opportunity-based rating proxies,
not these all-game variables. Its `ANALYSIS_COVERAGE.csv` keeps this gap visible.
An account-year external-marker merger is not part of this version.

## 0.3 Ordinary-game trajectories and engine calibration

For each ordinary, non-disconnection game retain the complete move trajectory,
ratings, speed, result and engine settings. A game that reaches a cell should
contribute once to that cell per oriented player, not once for every repeated
position in that cell. Choose first entry before calculating frequencies.
Wins/games estimate P(chooser wins); draws count as nonwins for this probability,
not as half a win. Rating gap is chooser minus opponent and cp is chooser POV.
Use floor(cp/50) and floor(gap/50), the fixed ply bands, and speed. Game IDs in
calibration must be disjoint from focal disconnection choices.

Separate results based on analysis-requested evaluated games from the random
sample; do not relabel the former representative. The planned independent
sample is 1,000,000 ordinary-game IDs chosen by deterministic seed/hash before
outcomes are used, with a sampling manifest and sampling probabilities. Low
budget is fixed at 2,000 nodes per sampled position, plus a deterministic 1%
validation subset at 100,000 nodes. Record Stockfish binary SHA, version,
UCI parameters, FEN/ply, score POV, depth/nodes and mate convention. Processing
all reached positions of 1M games can mean tens of millions of engine searches.
The engine workload therefore needs its own resumable, tested builder after
confirming the actual local PGN and evaluation formats.

Cells need >=100 distinct games. Report missing/unsupported cells, reliability
against the deeper validation subset, and coverage by focal state/stage/speed.
Keep selected-evaluation and random-evaluation calibrations in separate runs.
Do not stack duplicate cells from both samples into one lookup. Final
inference should resample calibration games and focal chooser accounts jointly;
the provided D1 conditional SE is a diagnostic, not that final uncertainty.

## F: probable waiting frame

Need every eligible rated pool/nonadjacent outoftime loss, with final
loser-oriented engine evaluation comparable to the main 100k-node evaluation,
last recorded loser clock, identities and time. This frame is not the current
active-timeout panel. Apply the same game/source/nonadjacency scope upstream;
retain a provenance audit of retained and rejected games, missing clocks and
missing evaluations. New chooser IDs must use the main panel's ID namespace.
Outoftime does not establish disconnection or deliberate waiting.

## D2: complete-game accuracy

Need pre/post-move engine scores for every decision by both players, with
consistent player POV and engine budget. Compute ACPL using a declared
nonnegative-loss and mate-score convention; export counted/evaluated moves
and missingness. The adapter requires complete paths. Hashes alone do not
prove completeness. Last-position and last-four-ply caches cannot substitute.

## Optional adapter contract

Place a separately audited manifest at:
`derived/replication/taxonomy_extended_inputs/EXTENDED_INPUTS.json`.
This file is included in the run key. Supplying it creates a distinct run;
it does not mutate a completed analysis. Do not set verified=true until a
builder's game counts, exclusions, engine provenance and coverage are checked.

Top-level object:

```json
{
  "contract": "LICHESS_TAXONOMY_EXTENDED_INPUTS_V1",
  "author_exclusion": ["__PRIVATE_VALUE_1__", "__PRIVATE_VALUE_2__"],
  "datasets": {}
}
```

Each dataset record contains `verified: true`, a `files` list of objects with
`path`, `sha256`, and optional `bytes`, plus the properties below. Paths can
be relative to the project or absolute within it. Files must be Parquet.
Builders must retain their own source/sampling/audit manifests locally.

| Dataset key | Required record properties | Required Parquet columns |
|---|---|---|
| waiting_events | scope=pool_nonadjacent_24m, cp_orientation=loser, engine_nodes=100000 | game_id, chooser, recipient, chooser_username, recipient_username, start_ms, cp, speed, loser_clock_s, status, rated |
| continuation_cells | probability_orientation=chooser, ordinary_games_only=true, game_disjoint_from_opportunities=true, cp_bin_width=50, rating_gap_bin_width=50, stage_upper_bounds=[2,4,6,10,20,40,80] | cp_bin_chooser, gap_bin_chooser, stage, speed, games, wins, sample_source |
| game_accuracy | scope=main_opportunity_games, complete_move_path=true, mate_convention=(explicit string) | game_id, chooser_acpl, recipient_acpl, chooser_moves, recipient_moves |

`chooser`/`recipient` are signed 64-bit IDs in the certified main namespace.
For waiting events recipient means the loser and chooser means the winner.
`start_ms` is UTC Unix milliseconds. `cp` is the final loser score, including
the same finite mate conversion as the main sample. Names are normalized and
checked against main identities and author exclusions. Waiting game IDs must
be unique and disjoint from active timeout choices. Missing loser clocks are
not selected as probable waiting. `status` must be outoftime, `rated` true.

Continuation cells must be unique across all supplied files. `stage` is a
0-based integer; upper-bound bands are inclusive. `games` and `wins` count
distinct games reaching each oriented cell. The program checks arithmetic
and uniqueness, but cannot establish random sampling from a claim in JSON.
`sample_source` must identify random versus analysis-requested calibration.

Accuracy IDs join to the certified main sample, ensuring author exclusion.
Both ACPLs are nonnegative finite values, with >=1 evaluated move per player.
The full-path claim and mate convention must be backed by builder audits.

If an adapter is missing, its stage is marked BLOCKED_INPUT and the remaining
local stages continue. A malformed or contradictory supplied input fails the
run rather than being silently treated as missing. Return the partial report
and log so the contract issue can be corrected at its source.
